    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The clinical benefits of public health interventions, like COVID-19 vaccines, are largely \r\ndocumented and understood, but little evidence is available to inform the human behavioral and \r\neconomic impacts of these interventions. This analysis utilizes anonymized cellphone and \r\npublicly available COVID-19 data to quantify the impact of COVID-19 health interventions on \r\ngeographic mobility in New York City. Mobility is defined as visit and foot traffic totals, \r\ncaptured through cellphone pings to business and retail locations, aggregated into weekly totals, \r\nfrom May 2019 – December 2021. The percent change in mobility between time intervals of \r\ninterest and a linear regression model inform the results of this analysis. The key findings \r\nsuggest that geographic mobility in New York City remains below pre-pandemic levels. In \r\naddition, COVID-19 vaccines do not appear to have a significant impact in increasing \r\ngeographic mobility. However, the conclusive impact of COVID-19 vaccines on geographic \r\nmobility cannot be quantified through this analysis alone. Additional analyses should be \r\nconducted to identify alternate factors influencing geographic mobility. These results and \r\nconclusions discussed can provide a new perspective for policymakers and public health officials \r\nto evaluate the non-clinical impacts of public health interventions. ",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ryschon, A. (2022). Big Shot for the Big Apple -- Can the COVID-19 Vaccine Mobilize the City that Never Sleeps? . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sx677dn3514",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
